        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Water reaches treadmills in more ways than people expect: a basement flood, a burst pipe,
          a roof leak above a garage, a knocked-over bottle on the console, years of sweat
          dripping onto the same spot on the frame. The question that brings most people here is
          whether the machine can be saved and what it will cost. The honest answer depends on
          three things — how much water, where it went, and whether the treadmill was powered
          while wet — and on what you do in the next few days. This page covers the immediate
          safety steps, how to dry a treadmill properly, which components water harms and how,
          estimated repair costs, how warranties and insurance typically treat water damage, and
          when replacing the machine is the better decision.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Can a Treadmill Be Fixed After Water Damage? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Often yes, if it was not switched on while wet and is dried out thoroughly
            before power goes back in. Do not turn it on to "see if it still works" — that is the
            single step most likely to turn a drying job into a repair bill.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Water on its own does relatively little to a treadmill's electronics in the short term.
            The damage comes from current flowing through wet circuits, which can short components
            on the motor control board or the console in an instant, and from corrosion and
            contamination that develop over days and weeks afterwards. So the two things that most
            decide whether a wet treadmill survives are whether it was powered while wet and how
            quickly and completely it was dried.
          </p>
          <p>
            Broadly:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A spill on the console or deck,</strong> wiped up quickly, usually needs
            nothing more than drying.</li>
            <li><strong>Water that got into the motor compartment</strong> but did not stand for
            long needs careful drying and an inspection before power goes back on.</li>
            <li><strong>Flood water that rose over the base</strong> is the serious case. The motor
            control board, motor, rollers and deck may all be affected, and flood water is
            usually dirty, which makes corrosion worse.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Repair costs range from nothing for a dried-out spill to the price of a control board
            and motor for a flooded machine, which on a budget treadmill can exceed what the
            machine is worth. The cost section below sets out estimated ranges, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a>
            covers the components in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What Should You Do First When a Treadmill Gets Wet?</h2>
<p>
            Make the electrics safe, then get the water away from the machine, then record what
            happened. In that order.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Do not switch it on.</strong> Not to test it, not to move the incline, not
            to fold it with powered assistance.</li>
            <li><strong>If the outlet, plug or surrounding floor is wet, switch off at the breaker
            first.</strong> Turn off the circuit at the panel, and only then unplug the treadmill.
            Do not reach for a plug in a wet outlet or stand in water to do it. If you cannot get to
            the panel safely, or water has reached it, call an electrician.</li>
            <li><strong>If everything around the plug is dry,</strong> switch off at the treadmill's
            power switch and unplug at the wall.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the safety key</strong> so nobody starts it by accident while it
            dries, and put a note on the console.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove standing water</strong> from around and under the machine, and if
            the treadmill is standing in water, move it to a dry area once the power is off — with
            two people, because it is heavy and wet floors are slippery.</li>
            <li><strong>Photograph everything</strong> before you clean up: the water level on the
            frame, the room, the source. If you might make an insurance claim, this record
            matters.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Then wipe the outside of the machine down and start drying it. The sooner it dries, the
            less time corrosion has to start. If the water came from a source that might be
            contaminated — flood water, a sewer backup — wear gloves and treat cleaning as a
            hygiene job as well as a drying one.
          </p>
          <p>
            One thing to resist: the urge to power it up after a day because it "looks dry". The
            outside of a treadmill dries quickly; the inside of the motor compartment, the console
            housing and the connectors do not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flood-spill-sweat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Does It Matter Whether It Was a Flood, a Spill or Sweat?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, a great deal. The amount of water, where it went, how long it stayed and how clean
            it was all change the outlook. The table is a general guide, not a diagnosis.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What happened</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Parts most at risk</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical outlook</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drink spilled on the console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console buttons and board</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually fine if powered off and dried; sugary drinks can leave sticky residue</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Water spilled on the deck or belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt underside, deck surface</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually fine; dry and check the lubricant</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Leak dripping from above</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console, motor compartment if under the hood</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Depends where it landed and for how long</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shallow water on the floor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frame feet, rear rollers, low wiring</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Often recoverable with drying and inspection</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Flood over the base</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Control board, motor, rollers, deck, wiring</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Serious; technician inspection before power</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sweat or drips over months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frame paint, fasteners, console buttons</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slow corrosion; preventable</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why flood water is worse than tap water</h3>
          <p>
            Flood water is rarely clean. It carries silt, debris and often sewage, and in coastal
            areas it may be salty. When it dries, it leaves deposits on circuit boards and inside
            connectors that can conduct, corrode or both, and those deposits do not leave just
            because the water has evaporated. Silt in a motor or bearing acts as a grinding paste.
            That is why a treadmill that sat in flood water deserves a professional inspection
            and cleaning before it is powered, even when it looks fine.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sweat is water damage in slow motion</h3>
          <p>
            Sweat is salty and slightly acidic, and it tends to fall in the same places every
            session: on the console, the handrails and the frame either side of the motor hood.
            Over months it lifts paint, rusts fasteners and gets into console buttons. It is not
            an emergency, but it is the commonest form of water damage on home treadmills, and a
            towel and a wipe-down after every session prevent nearly all of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How Do You Dry Out a Wet Treadmill Properly?</h2>
<p>
            Slowly, with air movement, unplugged, at room temperature, and for longer than seems
            necessary. A common rule of thumb is to allow several days in a warm, dry room before
            powering a treadmill whose electronics got wet, and longer after a flood; that is a
            convention rather than a tested figure, and an inspection is a better guide than the
            calendar.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Keep it unplugged</strong> throughout, with the safety key out.</li>
            <li><strong>Move it to a dry room</strong> if the area where it got wet is still damp.
            A garage or basement that flooded will stay humid for a while.</li>
            <li><strong>Use fans and a dehumidifier.</strong> Moving air and dry air together do
            more than heat.</li>
            <li><strong>Avoid direct heat.</strong> Heat guns, space heaters aimed at the machine
            and hair dryers on high can warp plastics and push moisture further into
            components.</li>
            <li><strong>Open what is easy to open.</strong> Raising the deck on a folding machine
            lets air under it. Lifting the belt edge lets the deck surface dry.</li>
            <li><strong>Consider the motor hood carefully.</strong> Removing it lets the motor
            compartment dry much faster. Only do so with the machine unplugged, after waiting for
            the control board's capacitors to discharge, and without touching the board. If you
            are not comfortable doing that, leave it for a technician.</li>
            <li><strong>Wipe metal parts dry</strong> — frame, rollers where accessible, fasteners —
            to slow the start of rust.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Do not rely on rice, desiccant packets or a sealed bag; a treadmill is far too large,
            and they do little that a dehumidifier does not do better.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the water was dirty, drying alone is not enough. Silt and contaminants left on the
            control board and in connectors need cleaning by someone who knows how, which usually
            means a technician. A board that dries with deposits on it can work at first and then
            fail weeks later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parts-at-risk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Which Parts of a Treadmill Does Water Damage?</h2>
<p>
            The electronics are most vulnerable to sudden failure; the metal and the deck are most
            vulnerable to slow damage. Going through the main components:
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Motor control board</h3>
          <p>
            The board under the motor hood converts mains power to drive the motor, and it is the
            most expensive electronic part on many machines. It sits low in the base, which puts it
            in the path of any flood. Powering it while wet risks shorting components; even if it
            was not powered, corrosion on its traces and connectors can cause faults later.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Console</h3>
          <p>
            The console is high up, so floods usually miss it, but spills and leaks from above do
            not. Membrane buttons can trap liquid underneath, and a touchscreen console is a
            sealed-ish computer that does not like water inside its housing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Drive motor</h3>
          <p>
            A motor that got wet and was not powered can often be dried and used. One that sat in
            dirty water may have silt in its bearings or contamination in the windings, and a DC
            motor's brushes and commutator do not respond well to either.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Rollers, bearings and frame</h3>
          <p>
            Roller bearings are sealed against dust rather than immersion, and water that gets in
            leads to rust and, later, a grinding or squealing noise. Rust can also form on the
            roller surfaces, the frame, the fasteners and the folding mechanism, particularly at
            cut edges and scratches in the paint.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deck</h3>
          <p>
            On most home treadmills the deck is a thick fibreboard or plywood panel with a hard
            coating on top. Fibreboard in particular swells when it soaks up water, and a swollen
            or delaminated deck is uneven, raises friction and usually means a replacement. A deck
            that was only splashed and dried quickly is generally fine.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Belt, wiring and the rest</h3>
          <p>
            A walking belt tolerates water but can wash its lubricant off or develop mould in
            the weave if left damp. Wiring connectors, the incline motor, the speed sensor and the
            safety key switch can all corrode. None of these is usually expensive alone, but they
            add up, and corroded connectors cause the intermittent faults that are hardest to
            trace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="switching-back-on" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. When Is It Safe to Switch a Wet Treadmill Back On?</h2>
<p>
            When it is completely dry inside and out, has been inspected, and is plugged into a
            dry, safe outlet. For anything beyond a minor spill, that inspection is best done by a
            technician; for a machine that sat in flood water, treat a technician's check as
            essential.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before power goes back in:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Check the outlet and circuit.</strong> If the outlet itself got wet, have
            it checked by an electrician before using it. An outlet with GFCI protection adds a
            useful layer of safety in any room that has flooded once.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the cord and plug</strong> for damage, corrosion on the pins or
            water in the cord inlet at the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Look inside the motor compartment,</strong> if you have opened it, for
            remaining moisture, tide marks, silt or corrosion. Any of these means it is not ready.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the deck</strong> for swelling and the rollers for rust.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For the first power-up, stand clear, keep nobody on the belt, and watch and smell. A
            burning or hot-electrical smell, smoke, crackling, a tripped breaker or an immediate
            shutdown means switch off and unplug, and do not try again. If it does not start at all,
            work through the basic checks in our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that will not turn on</a>
            before concluding the water killed it; the safety key, a tripped reset switch or a
            GFCI outlet that has not been reset are all common. If it trips the breaker, our guide
            to a
            <a href="/treadmill-trips-breaker/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that trips the breaker</a>
            explains how to tell a circuit problem from a machine fault — and after water damage, a
            machine fault is the likelier answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            If it runs, run it slowly with nobody on it for a few minutes, then walk on it at low
            speed before using it normally. Listen for new noise from the rollers and motor over the
            next few weeks; bearing damage from water often shows up later rather than at once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How Much Does It Cost to Repair a Water-Damaged Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Anywhere from nothing to more than the treadmill is worth, depending on which parts
            failed. The figures below are estimated ranges for home treadmills in the United States,
            consistent with the ranges in our repair guide. They are not quotes: parts prices vary
            widely by brand and model, labour varies by area, and premium machines sit above these
            ranges for parts.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Item</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Part, estimated</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">With a technician, estimated all in</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Inspection and cleaning visit</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Call-out of roughly $75-$150, plus labour</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor control board</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$150-$450</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$300-$700</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console board</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$150-$500, more for touchscreens</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$300-$800</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$200-$600</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$350-$900</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$100-$300</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$300-$600</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$80-$250</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$250-$500</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rollers or bearings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Varies widely by model</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Ask for a quote with the part</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Putting those together gives three rough scenarios. A <strong>spill that dried
            out</strong> commonly costs nothing, or a bottle of lubricant if the belt was washed
            dry. <strong>Water in the motor compartment that was caught early</strong> may need
            only an inspection and cleaning visit, or a single board. A <strong>flood over the
            base</strong> can mean a control board, a motor, a deck and a belt, which taken
            together can run well past a thousand dollars on these estimates — more than many
            budget and some mid-range treadmills are worth.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ask for the inspection first and a written quote second, and if the technician finds
            multiple water-damaged parts, price the whole repair before agreeing to any of it. If
            the belt is the only casualty, our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-replacement-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt replacement cost</a>
            breaks that job down, including when it is reasonable to do yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="insurance-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Will the Warranty or Home Insurance Cover Water Damage?</h2>
<p>
            The manufacturer's warranty typically will not; home insurance sometimes will,
            depending on the cause of the water and your policy. Check both rather than
            assuming.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The manufacturer's warranty</h3>
          <p>
            Treadmill warranties cover defects in materials and workmanship. Water damage is not a
            defect, and warranties typically exclude damage from accidents, misuse and the
            environment the machine is kept in. Many manuals specify a dry, climate-controlled
            indoor location, and use somewhere the manual excludes can void cover on its own. Read
            your warranty's exclusions, and if there is any doubt — a leak from a fault in the
            machine itself, for example — ask the manufacturer. Opening the machine or having an
            unauthorised repair done can also affect remaining warranty cover, so ask before work
            starts if the machine is still within its terms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Homeowners and renters insurance</h3>
          <p>
            Policies differ, and the cause of the water usually decides it. As a general pattern,
            standard homeowners policies typically cover sudden and accidental water damage from
            inside the home, such as a burst pipe, while flooding from outside — rising water from
            storms or rivers — is typically excluded and needs separate flood insurance. Gradual
            leaks and long-term seepage are often excluded too. Renters insurance may cover your
            belongings on similar terms. Your policy wording is what counts, so read it or ask your
            insurer.
          </p>
          <p>
            Practical points for a claim:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Photograph before cleaning up,</strong> as described above.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the damaged parts</strong> until the claim is settled.</li>
            <li><strong>Get a written repair quote or assessment</strong> from a technician.</li>
            <li><strong>Find the receipt</strong> or proof of purchase and the model number.</li>
            <li><strong>Weigh the deductible.</strong> If the treadmill is the only loss and its
            value is close to the deductible, a claim may not be worth making, and claims can affect
            premiums.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Should You Repair a Water-Damaged Treadmill or Replace It?</h2>
<p>
            Use the same rule of thumb as for any treadmill repair, with an extra margin for
            uncertainty. If the quoted repair is well under half of what a comparable used machine
            is worth today, repair is usually sensible. If it approaches or exceeds the machine's
            current value, replace. Water damage tilts the decision towards replacement more than
            a single worn part would, for three reasons.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Hidden damage.</strong> Corrosion on connectors and boards, and water in
            bearings, can cause faults weeks or months after a repair that seemed to work. The
            first repair bill may not be the last.</li>
            <li><strong>Multiple parts at once.</strong> A flood rarely damages only one component.
            Price the board, motor, deck and rollers together, not one at a time.</li>
            <li><strong>Insurance may change the arithmetic.</strong> If the loss is covered, the
            policy's terms, and whether it pays replacement cost or depreciated value, may make
            the decision for you.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            On a budget treadmill that sat in flood water, replacement is usually the realistic
            answer. On a premium machine with a sound frame, a professional clean, a new control
            board and new rollers can be well worth it. Between those, get an inspection and a full
            quote before deciding.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are replacing, dispose of the old machine responsibly; scrap metal recyclers and
            municipal bulk collection usually take treadmills. And whatever you decide, do not sell
            or give away a flood-damaged treadmill without telling the buyer. A machine that runs
            today but has corroded electronics is a fault waiting to happen, and the next owner
            deserves to know.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-flooded" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. How Can You Tell If a Used Treadmill Has Been Water Damaged?</h2>
<p>
            Look low down and inside. Flood damage leaves marks that cosmetic cleaning rarely
            removes completely, and a used treadmill from a basement or garage is worth checking
            carefully before you pay for it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Rust on the frame feet, lower fasteners and roller ends,</strong> especially
            if the upper parts of the frame are clean. Rust that stops at a consistent height is a
            tide mark.</li>
            <li><strong>Silt, dried mud or a water line</strong> inside the motor compartment, if
            the seller will let you look with the machine unplugged.</li>
            <li><strong>A swollen, uneven or delaminating deck,</strong> particularly at the edges
            and the rear.</li>
            <li><strong>Grinding or squealing rollers</strong> when the belt runs, which can point
            to water in the bearings.</li>
            <li><strong>Corrosion on the cord plug pins</strong> or at the cord inlet.</li>
            <li><strong>A musty smell</strong> from the belt or under the hood.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Ask directly where the machine was kept and whether it has ever been wet. If the answer
            is a basement or garage, the conditions matter; our guide to keeping a
            <a href="/treadmill-in-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill in a garage</a>
            explains how damp and condensation affect a machine even without a flood. A seller who
            will not let you see it run, or will not answer, is giving you an answer of a kind.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Preventing water damage in the first place</h3>
          <p>
            Keep the treadmill off floors that flood, away from windows and pipes that leak, and
            out of unheated spaces where condensation forms. A mat under the machine protects the
            floor but will not keep flood water out, so raising the location matters more than
            raising the machine. Keep drinks in the console bottle holder rather than on top of
            the console, wipe sweat off the frame and console after each session, and plug into an
            outlet with GFCI protection in any room that has ever been damp.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a treadmill be fixed after water damage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes, particularly if it was not switched on while wet and was dried thoroughly before power went back in. A spill on the console or deck usually needs only drying. A treadmill that sat in flood water may need its control board, motor, rollers or deck replaced, and should be inspected by a technician before it is powered.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to repair a water-damaged treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As an estimate, from nothing for a spill that dries out, to a call-out of roughly $75 to $150 plus labour for an inspection, to $300 to $700 for a replacement motor control board fitted. A flood that damages the board, motor, deck and belt can exceed a thousand dollars, more than many budget treadmills are worth.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I turn on my treadmill to see if it still works after it got wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Powering a treadmill while its electronics are wet is the step most likely to cause real damage, because current through wet circuits can short components instantly. Keep it unplugged with the safety key out, dry it for several days in a warm, dry room, and have it inspected before power goes back in if water reached the motor compartment.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a treadmill warranty cover water damage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically not. Warranties cover manufacturing defects, and water damage is usually excluded as accidental or environmental damage. Use in a location the manual excludes, such as a damp space, can also affect cover. Read the exclusions and ask the manufacturer if the water came from a fault in the machine itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does homeowners insurance cover a flooded treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the cause and the policy. Standard homeowners policies typically cover sudden accidental water damage from inside the home, such as a burst pipe, but typically exclude flooding from outside, which needs separate flood insurance. Photograph the damage, keep the parts, get a written assessment, and weigh the value against your deductible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is sweat bad for a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over time, yes. Sweat is salty and slightly acidic and tends to drip onto the same spots on the console, handrails and frame, where it lifts paint, rusts fasteners and gets into buttons. It is slow damage rather than an emergency, and wiping the machine down with a towel after each session prevents most of it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With a Wet Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most water-damaged treadmills that end up beyond repair got there through what happened after the water, not the water itself.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Switching it on to see if it works</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Current through wet circuits can short the control board or console in an instant, turning a drying job into a repair bill. Keep the treadmill unplugged with the safety key out until it is completely dry inside and has been inspected, however dry the outside looks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Unplugging from a wet outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Reaching for a plug in a wet outlet, or standing in water to do it, is a shock risk. Switch the circuit off at the breaker panel first, and only if you can reach it without standing in water. If water has reached the panel, keep out and call an electrician.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drying with direct heat and calling it done in a day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Heat guns and heaters aimed at the machine can warp plastics without drying the inside of the motor compartment or the connectors. Use fans and a dehumidifier in a warm room, allow several days as a rule of thumb, and treat an inspection as a better guide than the calendar.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating flood water like a spill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Flood water carries silt, contaminants and sometimes salt, which stay on circuit boards and in bearings after the water evaporates. A board that dries with deposits on it can work at first and fail weeks later. Have a flooded treadmill professionally inspected and cleaned before powering it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

