        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">A wet treadmill can often be saved, as long as nobody switches it
            on while it is wet.</strong> Make the electrics safe at the breaker if the outlet is
            wet, unplug, remove the key, dry it slowly for days rather than hours, and have it
            inspected before power goes back in if water reached the motor compartment.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A spill usually costs nothing; a flood over the base can cost more than a budget machine
            is worth, and warranties typically exclude it. Get an inspection and a full quote
            before deciding, and use the repair-or-replace rule of thumb in our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill repair guide</a>
            to make the call.
          </p>
        </div>
